\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  You said you\'92re familiar with attachment styles. How often did you experience anxiety or emotional overwhelm in relationships?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Funny enough, I\'92m in a different attachment style now. I feel secure. But for your project, I can talk from my past, when I was anxious\'97if that\'92s useful.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  That would be great.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  I used to be very anxious. I\'92m not sure if what changed me was time, growth, and self-work\'97or meeting the right partner. I kept attracting emotionally unavailable men while I wanted deep, life-affirming partnership. Of course that triggered anxiety.\
And here\'92s a nuance: it\'92s easy to say, \'93Maybe he isn\'92t the wrong partner, maybe I\'92m the problem and should fix my anxious style.\'94 But the point of knowing your attachment style or love languages isn\'92t to demand, \'93I need words of affirmation, so give them to me.\'94 If someone learns what you need and still won\'92t try, maybe they\'92re not your person. Ideally you both try to meet each other\'92s needs.\
My pattern was ignoring people\'92s true colors. I\'92d wait and hope they\'92d become someone who could meet me\'97usually they didn\'92t. With the right partner, your attachment can feel different.\
For your product: if someone were already secure, they wouldn\'92t need emergency breathing exercises. So it\'92s valuable to help people regulate their own nervous system.\
As a mother, I see this differently now. My job is to regulate my baby\'92s nervous system, which means I have to regulate mine first. Yesterday he started gagging on a carrot; the instinct is to panic, but that dysregulates him more. First I breathe and calm down, then I act. So your daily practice track might also support moms\'97not just people dating.\
I still get triggered, but now it\'92s more my son who dysregulates me. And remember: there\'92s also the avoidant end of the spectrum. Both anxious and avoidant people are dealing with the same trigger; they just cope differently. The anxious response looks like, \'93Why aren\'92t you texting me? I can\'92t sleep, I\'92m clenching my jaw.\'94 The avoidant response is, \'93I can\'92t deal, I\'92m out\'97cigarette, distraction, flee.\'94 A tool that brings either style toward the middle\'97regulated enough to stay present and communicate\'97would be powerful.\
Also, improvement isn\'92t just distraction like hanging with friends until he texts. It\'92s: can you sit with yourself\'97journal, cook, do a craft\'97shift focus without avoidance? That\'92s real growth.\
From a business lens: lots already exists around \'93emotional regulation.\'94 Your unique angle might be attachment-style-informed regulation. Market positioning will matter.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  So maybe the broader message is nervous system regulation, with attachment-style intelligence behind the design.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Exactly. And think beyond dating. New moms. Even people who tend toward avoidance. The core promise is the same: regulate first so you don\'92t self-sabotage.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  When you think back to your earlier relationships\'97those anxious times\'97what did you actually do to help yourself? How did you cope?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Honestly? A lot of distraction. Traveling was my therapy. I vented to friends, processed everything out loud\'97that part I\'92m good at\'97but mostly I looked for solitude.\
My coping style was hyper-independence. When I felt desperate for someone\'92s attention, I\'92d force myself to prove I didn\'92t need them. Instead of going to a yoga class like a normal person, I\'92d fly to Kazakhstan alone. I\'92d tell myself, \'93I don\'92t need your text; I\'92m in Almaty living my life.\'94\
That worked for survival but created new problems. When I finally met someone with real potential\'97my husband\'97I had to relearn vulnerability and allow myself to 
\f2\i need
\f1\i0  again.\

